Build an automated model-calibration system for <APP/REPO>.

CONTEXT
- The app makes predictions using <MODEL/LIBRARY>, whose behaviour is controlled by
  tunable settings: <e.g. model family, order/hyperparameters, distribution, training
  window length, scaling factor>.
- Those settings are currently <hardcoded / guessed / set once by hand>.
- Historical ground truth is available in <DATA SOURCE>.
- Entities to calibrate over: <e.g. all tickers / users / regions>. If they fall into
  groups that plausibly behave differently (<e.g. stocks vs crypto>), calibrate each
  group independently rather than assuming one shared optimum.

GOAL
Empirically choose the settings that make predictions match reality best, prove the
choice is not luck, and write it back into config automatically.

METHOD — these are requirements, not suggestions

1. SCORE ONLY WHAT THE SETTINGS ACTUALLY AFFECT.
   Before writing the scorer, verify the metric changes when the setting changes.
   If part of the output is produced by a component the settings don't touch, any
   metric over that part is identical for every candidate and cannot rank anything.
   Print the metric for 2-3 very different configs and confirm the numbers differ.

2. EVALUATE OUT OF SAMPLE, WALK-FORWARD.
   Pick a past cutoff, train only on data before it, predict forward, compare to what
   actually happened. Repeat across many cutoffs (aim for 50+) and all entities.

3. USE A PROPER SCORING RULE for the quantity being predicted, not a convenience
   metric. State why the chosen loss is appropriate when the ground-truth signal is
   noisy. Do NOT rank on in-sample fit criteria (AIC/BIC/R²/log-likelihood) — those
   measure fit, not forecasting.

4. SOLVE NUISANCE PARAMETERS IN CLOSED FORM where the math allows, instead of grid
   searching. Judge every candidate at ITS OWN optimum so no candidate is penalised
   by a default that happened to suit another.

5. SEARCH THE FULL SPACE the library supports, not a token subset. Enumerate it,
   verify each combination actually runs end to end, and drop only what genuinely
   fails. Include options a human wouldn't have thought to try.

6. GUARD AGAINST SELECTION OVERFITTING — the critical part. Picking the best of N
   candidates means the winner is partly winning by luck.
   a. Hold out the most recent ~25% of evaluation points. Select on the rest.
      Fit any scale/nuisance parameter on the selection portion and apply it
      UNCHANGED to the holdout — refitting there leaks the answer.
   b. Significance-test the winner against THE CURRENT PRODUCTION SETTING (not an
      arbitrary baseline) on holdout data, using per-observation losses. Use an
      autocorrelation-robust standard error if evaluation windows overlap.
   c. Break statistical ties by simplicity (fewest parameters).
   d. Change config ONLY if the winner beats the incumbent on holdout AND clears
      p < 0.05. Otherwise keep the incumbent and say so explicitly.
      "No significant improvement" must be a supported, reported outcome.

7. REQUIRE FULL COVERAGE. A candidate that fails to converge on part of the universe
   is not eligible, however good its average looks.

8. BE REPRODUCIBLE. Fix seeds for any simulation. Same input, same ranking.

PLUMBING
- Verify the config schema can EXPRESS the winning answer and the serving code can
  SERVE it. Extend both first if not — a recommendation the app can't run is useless.
- Write results into a clearly delimited machine-managed block in the config file,
  then reload it to prove it still imports. Record provenance: date, score, p-value,
  entity count, parameter range.
- Calibrating one group must never clobber another group's settings.
- Persist full rankings to a dated file so runs can be diffed.
- Parallelise across entities; fetch each evaluation window's data once and reuse it
  for all candidates.

TESTS (required)
- Nuisance-parameter solver recovers a deliberately planted known value.
- The optimum genuinely beats neighbouring values.
- The significance test does NOT fire on pure noise (check false-positive rate).
- Every candidate in the search space runs and returns finite, valid output.
- Config write-back round-trips exactly and the result still imports.

DELIVERABLES
1. The calibration engine (also runnable for a single config, for debugging).
2. Config with a machine-managed block + provenance.
3. Tests above.
4. A runbook: the one command to run, expected runtime, how to read the output,
   and how to interpret "no change recommended".

FIRST, before writing code: review the existing setup and report what's wrong with
how these settings are chosen today. Be specific and skeptical — if the current
metric can't distinguish candidates, say so and show it.


---
NOTES ON WHY TWO POINTS ARE WORDED SO FORCEFULLY
(from the TickerWatcher GARCH calibration this was derived from)

Point 1 exists because the original sweep ranked eight volatility models on price-path
error — but the forecast price path came from historical mean drift, which the
volatility model does not influence at all. Every candidate scored an identical
"MAPE: 1.75%", and the tie silently fell through to an in-sample fit criterion.
The bug was invisible because the output looked perfectly reasonable.

Point 6 exists because a 5-window trial run, with no holdout and no significance test,
confidently recommended an exotic model (ARCH(3)) over the incumbent GARCH(1,1).
With 160 candidates and a small sample, something will always look like a winner.
Adding the holdout split plus a Diebold-Mariano test against the incumbent was what
made the recommendation trustworthy rather than merely confident.
